This skill's visual-type vocabulary — which types exist, what each one is
for, the proportions and complexity budget that keep an instance readable,
and the anti-patterns each type invites — is derived from the diagram-design
skill by Cathryn Lavery, used under the MIT licence reproduced below.

The implementation is this skill's own: the builders in scripts/viztypes.py
are written against svgkit's theme and primitives and emit Python-generated
SVG, where the original is a hand-authored HTML/SVG grammar. No source file
is copied verbatim.

    https://github.com/cathrynlavery/diagram-design

